<section>
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2>From the coach</h2>
      <p>You train seriously. You're up before the sun, you finish the intervals even when they burn, you put in the hours on the bike. You're building an engine.</p>
      <p>The problem is that most athletes I coach arrive with the engine well built and the fuel improvised. They rehearse pace. They rehearse cadence. They rehearse transitions. Nutrition gets decided the night before.</p>
      <p>This guide exists to close that gap. It won't tell you how to eat in daily life. It will tell you <strong>how much, when, and in what form to put fuel into and around your sessions</strong>, with numbers you can use tomorrow.</p>
      <p>One rule organises everything else: <strong>the gut is trainable, and nothing is new on race day.</strong></p>
      <p>The athlete who rehearses fuelling with the same discipline as intervals stops surviving races and starts racing them.</p>
      <p>Let's get to work.</p>
      <p><strong>Iván</strong><br>Founder &amp; Head Coach, Triaperformance</p>

